<Project>

  <!--
    Imported by every assembly Unity has to be able to load. ADR-0002.

    IMPORT THIS FROM INSIDE THE PROJECT BODY, not from Directory.Build.props:

        <Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
          <Import Project="../Shared/UnityBound.props" />
          ...

    WHY, and it is worth reading before "simplifying" this back:

    Directory.Build.props used to carry these settings behind a
    Condition="'$(DK2UnityBound)' == 'true'" that each project was supposed to
    switch on. It never worked, and nothing noticed for four months. MSBuild
    imports Directory.Build.props *before* the project body, so $(DK2UnityBound)
    was always empty when the condition was evaluated - every project, including
    every Unity-bound one, silently built as net10.0. Polyfills.cs sat behind
    #if NETSTANDARD2_1 and was never once compiled.

    ADR-0002 claimed netstandard2.1 was "enforced by the compiler from day one".
    It was not enforced at all, and the first thing that noticed was an attempt
    to actually compile against Unity's assemblies (2026-09-04). Unity cannot
    load a net10.0 assembly, so the entire Unity plan would have failed at the
    first attempt to use it.

    An import in the body evaluates after the SDK props and before the SDK
    targets, which is exactly when TargetFramework has to be set. That is why
    this is an explicit Import and not a condition.

    ArchitectureTests.UnityLoadableAssemblies_TargetNetStandard21 now asserts the
    result from the built assemblies, so a repeat cannot be silent.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>netstandard2.1</TargetFramework>
    <DK2UnityBound>true</DK2UnityBound>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Compiler shims for records, init accessors and required members, which
         netstandard2.1 does not provide. Guarded by #if NETSTANDARD2_1 in the
         file itself, so it is inert if this ever targets something newer. -->
    <Compile Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)Polyfills.cs" Link="Properties/Polyfills.cs" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
